 "spec": "Vocabulary ids: 0 <pad>, 1 <bos>, 2 <eos>, 3 ' ', 4 ',', 5 '.', 6 '!', 7 '?', 8 \"'\", 9 '\"', 10..35 = a..z (a=10, e=14, i=18, o=24, u=30, y=34). Input ctx [8,36]: one-hot rows, oldest first, row 7 = most recent token; an all-zero row means 'before document start'. LETTER row = token in 10..35. BOUNDARY row = an all-zero row, or a row whose token is not a letter and is not the apostrophe (id 8) [so bos, space, , . ! ? and the double quote are boundaries; the apostrophe is NOT]. 'word so far' = the maximal run of LETTER rows ending at row 7 (or at the row named in the rule); it is 'anchored' when the row just before the run exists and is a BOUNDARY row. Evidence output y [36] is added to next-token logits by the parent, in nats; 0 means 'no opinion'. Entries for ids 0,1 must be exactly 0 unless a rule says otherwise; id 2 (<eos>) is 0 unless a rule says otherwise. Case 'note' fields explain the case; 'expect' vectors are index 0..35 in vocabulary order and must be matched within tol. Inputs are exact one-hot / 0-1 values. Tables in 'data' are authoritative and must not be changed. You may write a helper script that expands 'data' into proposal JSON. Each cell/param holds at most 40 numbers (spread lookups over many cells and combine with ops; ops on ctx rows such as slice/mul/sum are exact and free of parameters).\n\nName-cache reader. Inputs ctx [8,36] and nm [224] (layout exactly as in cl_name_write). Output y [36] evidence. Let cap = nm[216], L = argmax of nm[217..223], buffer B = nm[0..215] as 6x36,\nname[i] = argmax of B[i] for i < L. If cap >= 0.5 or L < 2: y = zeros. Otherwise, with p = token in row 7:\n * p is space (3) or double quote (9) (a word is about to start): y[name[0]] = 1.2.\n * p is a letter: let w = anchored word-so-far (run of letters ending at row 7, anchored by a BOUNDARY row, k = len(w) <= L). If w equals name[0:k]: if k < L (and k <= 5): y[name[k]] = g_k with g_1 = 1.5, g_2 = 2.5, g_3 = 3.0, g_4 = 3.0, g_5 = 3.0;\n   if k == L: y[' '] = 1.5, y[','] = 0.7, y['.'] = 0.7, y[\"'\"] = 0.6, y['!'] = 0.3, y['?'] = 0.2. Otherwise (mismatch, k > L, or not anchored): zeros.\n * any other p: zeros.\nIds 0,1,2 are 0. Inputs are always valid states. Dot products of buffer rows with ctx rows give the letter-match tests (ops), plus a few small cells or scale ops.",
